    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No problem at all; we can definitely flesh out the wrist pain section. It's important to give them actionable advice, not just general advice. I've expanded the section to offer more specific guidance on keyboard and wrist support.\n\n***\n\n**Subject: Tips for a Healthier Remote Workspace: Ergonomics Matters!**\n\nTeam,\n\nAs we continue to work remotely, we want to ensure everyone is set up for comfort and health. We've noticed some reports of wrist pain and back aches, so we're sharing some quick ergonomic tips to help prevent discomfort.\n\n**Focusing on Wrist Health:**\nFor those experiencing wrist discomfort, pay close attention to your keyboard setup. Your wrists should remain in a neutral, straight position while typing—avoid bending them up or down excessively. Consider using a proper wrist rest, but remember that the rest is for *pausing* between typing, not while actively striking keys. Your keyboard should be positioned so your elbows are close to your body and bent at about a 90-degree angle.\n\n**General Setup Tips:**\nRemember to adjust your chair height so your feet are flat on the floor, and ensure your monitor is at eye level to avoid straining your neck. Taking",
      "source": "target",
